#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152949} :activity/id #uuid "97e38bef-c381-502b-bf22-c707366554d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050678 :topic/id #uuid "5e4a6855-8fc5-54a9-98ee-f20765ff4e16" :topic/math-academy-id 2674} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153451 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 7 & ≡9\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5.$\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z},$\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3e0278c-44da-546e-bc47-9529380f4b5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9017 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "150a8841-cd4a-55a0-9103-03b339daec90" :step/math-academy-id 154615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151856 :question/id #uuid "aa58ae42-bdf6-5303-9c9c-2e20151f2ed8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14610"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413586}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"} :step/id #uuid "eb1b2b70-7fdf-523c-bc68-d9bd60ecf54c" :step/math-academy-id 154616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153463 :tutorial/content "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "5778b7fb-626e-59d3-ad81-8d574bb2b4e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9040 :tutorial/title "The Multiplicative Property"} :step/id #uuid "25406990-f673-5af1-86df-1fa7639c14ee" :step/math-academy-id 154619 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151840 :question/id #uuid "af294c61-bf5b-5b8a-a104-853ea1418cb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049647}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413585}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"} :step/id #uuid "7c24bd18-6dc6-5fee-9d4f-4f05d01d4776" :step/math-academy-id 154620 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151841 :question/id #uuid "043fcc75-20fe-5027-8585-ab24d45ac89d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413584}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "a4cc2be4-a3b1-5aa1-8676-c62d501d2546" :step/math-academy-id 154623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153469 :tutorial/content "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).$ By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain \n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5dad198-84e6-5eab-9d7e-6df354768fce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9076 :tutorial/title "The Exponentiation Property"} :step/id #uuid "deefc65a-57e3-571f-a62b-93fbf75d0ee8" :step/math-academy-id 154627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151864 :question/id #uuid "21d3a5de-63f0-5150-9cb8-c2cbc609bb8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413580}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"} :step/id #uuid "aaa53ec0-7f0e-5c35-81f0-63d71abae912" :step/math-academy-id 154628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153473 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following properties:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's prove the first property. The proof of the second property is similar.\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the scalar multiplication property from the definition of congruence. First, note that we have the following chain of implications for $k, p\\in\\mathbb Z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟹\\,a-b=pn \\\\  & ⟹\\,k(a-b)=kpn \\\\  & ⟹\\,ka-kb=(kp)n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $kp = q\\in\\mathbb Z.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka-kb=(kp)n\\, & ⟹\\,ka-kb=qn \\\\  & ⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof of the second property is very similar.\n\n- We'll prove the multiplicative and exponentiation properties in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "5cf4e7b1-34e6-55ea-bb8f-ae13a8fa29d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9125 :tutorial/title "Proving the Scalar Multiplication Property"} :step/id #uuid "82d98a08-ff41-586d-97b2-d5622638b737" :step/math-academy-id 154631}] :activity/title "The Multiplication Properties of Modular Arithmetic" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152341}]}
